According to the NHS, iron is needed to make haemoglobin, a substance in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body, and is also important for building and maintaining healthy muscles and for many of the body's functions and reactions (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). A Whittington NHS Trust patient factsheet additionally states that iron helps build blood cells, carries oxygen, supports enzyme function and immune system function, supports detoxification, and supports growth and development (https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=7). The NHS lists food sources of iron as including liver, kidney, red meat, sardines, wholegrain and fortified breakfast cereals, lentils, kidney beans, baked beans, chickpeas, dark green vegetables such as spinach and broccoli, nuts and seeds, dried apricots, and tofu (https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/iron-diet). The NHS states that the daily reference intake for men aged 19 and over is 8.7 mg per day, for women aged 19 to 49 is 14.8 mg per day, and for women aged 50 and over is 8.7 mg per day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron). The NHS states that taking 17 mg or less per day of iron supplements is unlikely to cause any harm, that side effects of high doses over 20 mg include constipation, feeling sick, being sick, and stomach pain, and that very high doses of iron can be fatal (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/iron).

