**Vitamin K — Sourced Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin K is a group of vitamins the body needs for blood clotting and helping wounds to heal, and that there is some evidence it may help keep bones healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). South Tees Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust describes vitamin K as essential for the formation of coagulation factors that work together to clot the blood when injuries to blood vessels occur (https://www.southtees.nhs.uk/services/pathology/tests/vitamin-k-profile-vit-k-pivka11). The NHS Health Research Authority additionally notes that vitamin K is important for the healthy function of several body systems, including blood vessels, bone, nerves, and muscle function (https://www.hra.nhs.uk/planning-and-improving-research/application-summaries/research-summaries/vitamin-k-to-improve-sway-in-older-people-k-sway).

The NHS lists food sources of vitamin K as green leafy vegetables such as broccoli and spinach, vegetable oils, and cereal grains, with small amounts also found in meat and dairy foods (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k). NHS Lanarkshire additionally lists kale, cabbage, and fortified breakfast cereals (https://www.nhslanarkshire.scot.nhs.uk/patient-information-leaflets/nutrition-dietetics/pil-fodsrc-21-12986-l).

The NHS states that adults need approximately 1 microgram of vitamin K per kilogram of body weight per day — for example, 65 micrograms per day for a person weighing 65 kg. Regarding supplements, the NHS states that taking 1 mg or less of vitamin K supplements a day is unlikely to cause any harm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-k).

NHS formularies list vitamin K medicines — including phytomenadione and menadiol sodium phosphate — as prescription medicines managed within NHS clinical frameworks (https://bswformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09.06.06&SubSectionID=A100; https://www.bedsformulary.nhs.uk/chaptersSubDetails.asp?FormularySectionID=9&SubSectionRef=09&SubSectionID=A100).

