**Zinc – Evidence Summary**

According to the NHS, zinc contributes to making new cells and enzymes, processing carbohydrate, fat and protein in food, and wound healing (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). Newcastle Hospitals NHS Laboratories further describe zinc as an essential trace element and a cofactor for over 300 enzymes, involved in energy and nucleic acid metabolism, immune function, wound healing, and tissue repair (https://laboratories.newcastle-hospitals.nhs.uk/test-directory/zinc-serum). North West London Pathology (NHS) adds that zinc is a component of many enzymes, required for transcription factors, antioxidant function, the immune system, respiration, neurological function, and normal growth and maturation (https://www.nwlpathology.nhs.uk/test/zinc).

The NHS lists good dietary sources as meat, shellfish, dairy foods such as cheese, bread, and cereal products such as wheatgerm (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). The Whittington NHS Trust also includes legumes, nuts, and seeds among the best sources (https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=3).

The NHS states that the amount of zinc needed is approximately 9.5 mg a day for men aged 19 to 64 and 7 mg a day for women (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others).

Regarding supplementation, the NHS states: "Do not take more than 25 mg of zinc supplements a day unless advised to by a doctor." The NHS also states that taking high doses of zinc reduces the amount of copper the body can absorb, which can lead to anaemia and weakening of the bones (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others). NHS 111 Wales publishes the same upper limit of 25 mg per day (https://111.wales.nhs.uk/vitaminsandminerals). The BNF holds a monograph for zinc sulfate as a named drug, indicating it exists as a licensed medicinal product in the UK (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/zinc-sulfate).

