**Zinc** is described by the NHS as an essential trace element involved in making new cells and enzymes, processing carbohydrate, fat and protein in food, and wound healing ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others)). Newcastle Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust further describes it as a cofactor for over 300 enzymes, involved in energy and nucleic acid metabolism, immune function, wound healing, and tissue repair ([Newcastle Hospitals Laboratories](https://laboratories.newcastle-hospitals.nhs.uk/test-directory/zinc-serum)). North West London Pathology (an NHS service) adds that zinc is a component of many enzymes, required for transcription factors, antioxidant function, immune system, respiration, and neurological function, and is crucial for normal growth and maturation ([NW London Pathology](https://www.nwlpathology.nhs.uk/test/zinc)).

Good food sources listed by the NHS include meat, shellfish, dairy foods such as cheese, bread, and cereal products such as wheatgerm ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others)). The NHS states the reference intake for zinc is approximately 9.5 mg a day for men aged 19 to 64, and 7 mg a day for women ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others)). NHS 111 Wales states that people should not take more than 25 mg of zinc supplements a day unless advised to by a doctor ([NHS 111 Wales](http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals)). The NHS also states that taking high doses of zinc reduces the amount of copper the body can absorb ([NHS](https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/others)).

